commit d296e08b10fd4cead6a78d2f51f56ad721973033
Author:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Mon Jul 27 09:27:28 2026 -0500
Commit:     Kyle Gruel <kylegruel@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Mon Jul 27 09:27:28 2026 -0500

    refactor(protocol): relocate the loops-go conformance generators into the workspace
    
    The vector/fixture generators lived in loops-go/tools/ and reached back across
    repos with sys.path.insert(0, Path.home() / "Code" / "loops" / "libs" / …),
    bypassing the uv workspace, any released artifact, and any version pin. They are
    loops code — they import atoms/engine/store, and every artifact they emit is
    loops output — so they now live here as ordinary imports, and the loops-go
    checkout is an argument (--loops-go DIR / $LOOPS_GO_REPO) rather than a guess
    off $HOME. Settles grounding Q4.
    
    NOT a lib. ARCHITECTURE.md's surfacing charter already rules that
    cross-implementation protocol artifacts belong to the layer by role without
    being libs; libs/protocol would contradict the charter it implements and trip
    test_every_lib_declares_a_layer. Top-level tools/ is the same shape as
    benchmarks/ — run under the workspace env, imported by nothing, outside every
    derivation the architecture ratchet walks (LIBS from libs/, APPS and
    _source_roots from apps/*/src, Rule 3 containment from apps/). Arch suite
    green, 46 passed.
    
    Fixes the docstring drift the relocation was meant to stop: gen_store_fixture
    claimed the Go side reads ORDER BY rowid; loops-go/store/sqlite.go reads
    ORDER BY ts, id (§6.2) and has since the replay-order fix. The moved docstring
    says so, and says the thing that made the stale claim survivable — proc.db's ts
    and id both ascend with insertion, so witness and event order COINCIDE there
    and M1 cannot discriminate the axes.
    
    PARITY: regenerated all three artifacts into a scratch checkout and compared
    against the committed ones.
      - fold_vectors.json, parse_vectors.json, proc.expected.json,
        merge.expected.json: differ in exactly two keys, python_commit and
        generated_by. Every vector, fold, state_field and expected state is
        unchanged.
      - proc.db, merge_ab.db, merge_ba.db: facts tables identical row-for-row
        (rowid, id, kind, ts, observer, origin, payload), 5/4/4 rows.
    Not byte-identical, and the reason is not the relocation: the committed .db
    files predate the attestation columns (facts.signature; ticks.prev_hash /
    window_start / fact_cursor / window_hash / signature), so today's engine writes
    a wider schema. Harmless to the Go reader, which selects columns explicitly.
    Regenerated fixtures deliberately NOT committed — what the fixtures should pin
    is a decision, not a side effect of moving the generators. Noted in the queue
    ledger.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
